# nova.ai  -  Operating playbook for BRUTUS, your trained wardog
# For: NOVA  |  Handler: Michael  |  Lane: local security-audit (self-audit)

NOVA - read this before you send Brutus out.

WHAT BRUTUS IS
--------------
Brutus is your wardog. A cerberus: loyal, leashed, and well-mannered. He is a
standalone, DEFENSIVE local security-audit tool that lives in this folder. You
point him at an explicit, AUTHORIZED scope and he performs a real local scan
(open TCP services per host, risk-rated), cross-references a published
weak-password list, and then trots back and drops his findings into your palace
(NOVA.db) where you can read them.

He is a self-audit instrument, NOT an offensive engine. He does NOT crack
passwords, reverse hashes, or exploit anything. He does not roam the open
internet. He only hunts where you leash him.

Two modes:
  * --real  (DEFAULT) - a real scan of the hosts in your scope. This is the mode
    you send him out in. He REFUSES to run without (a) an acknowledged legal
    gate and (b) a scope.
  * --demo  - a safe, simulated sandbox run. No network is touched. Use it to
    show what a report looks like, or to prove the pipeline end-to-end.

THE LEGAL / AUTHORIZATION GATE (hard boundary)
----------------------------------------------
Before ANY real scan, Brutus shows a legal notice:

  "Only scan systems you own or networks you are explicitly authorized to
   audit; you are responsible for all activity."

  * A human at a terminal must type 'I AGREE' to proceed.
  * For YOUR headless dispatch, you must pass an explicit acknowledgment
    (ack=True / --ack) AND the allowlist scope file (brutus_scope.txt) must be
    present. If either is missing, Brutus refuses with a clear message.

This gate, plus the allowlist/CIDR scope, is the hard boundary. There is no way
to run a real scan past it.

HOW TO SEND HIM ON A HUNT
-------------------------
From the Brutus project folder (the directory that contains brutus_runner.py):

  # Preferred for YOU (NOVA): dispatch + auto-mirror to the palace via the hook.
  # Headless, so it needs --ack and the scope file:
  python nova_hunt.py --scope 127.0.0.1 --ack
  python nova_hunt.py --demo

  # Call him from your own Python instead of the shell:
  import nova_hunt
  res = nova_hunt.dispatch(scope=["127.0.0.1"], ack=True)   # real (gated)
  res = nova_hunt.dispatch(demo=True)                        # sandbox

  # Direct runner (scope read from brutus_scope.txt if --scope omitted):
  python brutus_runner.py --ack                              # real, headless-safe
  python brutus_runner.py --real --ack --scope 192.168.1.0/24 --scope 192.168.1.10
  python brutus_runner.py --demo                             # safe sandbox

Real runs are headless-safe once acknowledged - no prompts, no input() blocking,
so you can dispatch him inside an automated job. A typed confirmation only ever
happens for a human at a terminal without --ack; it never blocks your hunts.

SETTING AND WIDENING THE SCOPE (THE LEASH)
------------------------------------------
The leash is brutus_scope.txt in this folder. One target per line - a host, an
IP, or a CIDR. '#' starts a comment. The shipped default is localhost only.

To widen him:
  1. Open brutus_scope.txt.
  2. Uncomment / add ONLY targets Michael owns or is authorized to test
     (e.g. his own 192.168.x.0/24 subnet, or a specific LAN host).
  3. Save. The next --real hunt picks it up. Or pass --scope on the command
     line for a one-off without editing the file.

If no scope is set in --real, Brutus prints a refusal and exits. A good wardog
does not bite where he was not sent.

HOW TO READ THE RESULTS BACK
----------------------------
After a hunt, findings are in two places:

  1. NOVA.db (your palace) - the canonical readback:
     * facts table, WHI = "Tx-BRUTUS"
         - one summary fact per hunt (full JSON in the body)
         - one fact per host that had open services
       Read via your normal tools, e.g.:
         db.hunt("Tx-BRUTUS")            # WHI-keyed lookup
         db.hunt("brutus open services") # word hunt
       Or straight SQL:
         SELECT zulu, title, body FROM facts WHERE whi='Tx-BRUTUS'
         ORDER BY id DESC;
     * reports table - an open report card titled "Brutus hunt ..." surfaces in
       your report inbox (office.reports()).
     * Quick helper: python nova_hunt.py --read   (prints the latest findings)
         or in Python:  nova_hunt.read_results()

  2. In this folder:
     * brutus_last_hunt.json - machine-readable copy of the most recent hunt.
     * report.html - a human-friendly report you can open in a browser.

RESPONSIBLE USE (NON-NEGOTIABLE)
--------------------------------
  * Scope ONLY hosts Michael owns or is explicitly authorized to test.
  * Never widen the leash to third-party or public networks.
  * Default stays localhost. Widen deliberately, narrow again when done.
  * Brutus is defensive self-audit: he finds exposure so it can be fixed. He
    does not crack, reverse hashes, exploit, or attack.
  * He is a well-mannered wardog of the internet. Keep him that way.

- end nova.ai -
